From: Long Way Home <noreply@long-way-home.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 21/21] SP: the bridge's configstring table derives from MAX_CONFIGSTRINGS

The SP bridge stored the game's configstrings in a table sized by a literal `CS_MAX 1024`, while
`MAX_CONFIGSTRINGS` -- what the gamestate and the save format are sized against -- is 4096. I_SetCS
dropped any index at or above 1024 and said nothing, so a configstring numbered above the table
would disappear with no error and be blamed on the content (docs/evidence/engine-content-headroom.md,
docs/evidence/raise-the-gentity-ceiling.md both record this as the "wrong 1024").

Measured on this tree before the change: `+map borg1` prints "content: 104 configstrings of 4096
(bridge table CS_MAX 1024)". The table is now `SP_CS_MAX`, derived from `MAX_CONFIGSTRINGS`, so it
can never again disagree with the ceiling; the table the bridge holds covers every configstring the
game's layout is allowed to name (bg_public.h errors out on a layout above MAX_CONFIGSTRINGS). The
name change is not cosmetic: bg_public.h, included for the content blocks, already defines `CS_MAX`
(the game's own layout bound, CS_PLAYERS + MAX_CLIENTS), and the old `#define` silently redefined it
under the build's `-w`.

An out-of-range set now says so under `developer`, and an index past the old literal that lands says
so too, so the two states are distinguishable in the engine's own output. Logging only: with
`developer` unset (the default) I_SetCS stores and drops exactly as before, and no save changes (the
save writes only the non-empty entries it always did).
